  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 138 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA very moist south-southwest flow aloft will continue through \nFriday between an upper level high over Texas and an upper level \nlow to the northwest. Latest ACARS soundings show precipitable \nwater values\u00a0of 1.2 inches, about 220 percent of normal. The \nsoundings also show a long skinny CAPE profile of about 500 J/kg, \nwhich favors heavy rainfall. Storms will\u00a0move somewhat quickly, \nwhich will limit the heavy rain and flooding threat. Best chance \nfor thunderstorms through this evening will be east of I-25 where \nit's been mostly sunny the past few hours and temperatures have \nclimbed into the upper 70s to lower 80s. To the west of I-25, it's\nbeen cloudy most of the day with on and off light rain. \nTemperatures have been slow to warm with 1:00 PM readings in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s. Light rain showers are expected to linger \ninto the evening, with the radar showing additional showers lined \nup to the south-southwest.\n\nThe upper level low will slowly\u00a0travel eastward across southern \nIdaho and Wyoming this weekend. Moisture decreases, but remains \nwell above normal (140-180 percent of normal) through Sunday. Mid \nlevel temperatures will cool as the upper level\u00a0low nears the \nregion. MLCAPE reaches 600-1200 J/kg both days. Increasing \nwesterly flow aloft around the upper level low will help produce \ngood shear Saturday with 25-40 knots of bulk shear (0\u20136 km) and \n35-50 knots of bulk shear (0\u20136 km) Sunday.\u00a0 High temperatures are \nexpected to be mainly in the 70s with Sunday being a few degrees \ncooler, which could end up capping convection. The passage of a \nshortwave trough is expected to result in better \nshower/thunderstorm coverage on Saturday. Models hint at heavier \nrainfall over the northeast plains after 00Z Sunday.\n\nOn Monday, the upper level low will weaken as it exits the \nregion. A new upper level low/trough moves over California and the\nGreat Basin. In the meantime, an upper level ridge will be \ncentered over Texas. For Colorado, weak ridging is expected to \nprevail, resulting in dry conditions.\n\nFor Tuesday through Thursday, an upper-level ridge remains over\nthe south central and southeast part of the country, while\ntroughiness will be over the southwest. This pattern will allow\nshortwave troughs to lift northward out of Mexico/the eastern\nPacific and move across the region. There isn't much agreement yet\non the location and timing of the shortwave troughs, but there\nshould be a day or two with at least scattered showers and\nthunderstorms. Temperatures during this time frame will be near\nnormal with highs in the 70s across northeast Colorado.",
